    19	//
    20	// Design choices (differ from slack-transport.ts / email-transport.ts,
    21	// verified against n8n's CURRENT live docs 2026-07-01, not memory):
    22	//   - RAW fetch, NO n8n SDK, fetchImpl injected -- same as Slack/Email.
    23	//   - DIGEST-ONLY JSON body, every value re-sanitized here (same quarantine
    24	//     discipline as the other two adapters).
    25	//   - NO FIXED RESPONSE ENVELOPE: unlike Slack ({ok:false} on HTTP 200) or
    26	//     Resend ({id}/{name}), an n8n Webhook-trigger node's response is
    27	//     ENTIRELY WORKFLOW-DEFINED (docs.n8n.io/integrations/builtin/core-nodes/
    28	//     n8n-nodes-base.webhook/) -- "Immediately" returns a fixed message with
    29	//     no data, "When Last Node Finishes" returns whatever the workflow
    30	//     produced, and a "Respond to Webhook" node can return anything. There is
    31	//     NO {ok:true}/{id} we can rely on. So the fail-closed trap keys ONLY on
    32	//     the one thing n8n guarantees: HTTP STATUS. A non-2xx is a hard failure.
    33	//   - providerRef is ALWAYS SYNTHETIC (n8n-<idempotencyKey>), NEVER derived
    34	//     from response body content, by deliberate design (not an oversight --
    35	//     3 rounds of Codex cross-model review each found a new way reading the
    36	//     body for an "id" leaked data: an unbounded workflow-echoed string, the
    37	//     configured header credential echoed back, then a truncated/partial
