    44	// Keyed by `${routeKey}:${clientId}` so the three routes have independent budgets
    45	// (one noisy upload caller cannot exhaust the approve budget).
    46	const buckets = new Map<string, WindowState>();
    47	
    48	// HARD cap on distinct keys. A key-rotating caller (rotating a spoofable client id) could
    49	// otherwise grow the Map unbounded (Codex LOW). When bucket CREATION crosses this cap we first
    50	// sweep fully-elapsed windows (cheap, the common reclaim), and if that is not enough -- a burst
    51	// of DISTINCT still-ACTIVE keys, which pruneExpired cannot touch -- we EVICT the oldest windows
    52	// until we are back under the cap. So the Map size is genuinely bounded by MAX_TRACKED_KEYS, not
    53	// merely swept (EV-13, 2026-07-16 re-review: the prior sweep-only path was a SOFT cap that a
    54	// same-tick spoofed-id flood defeated).
    55	//
    56	// TRUSTED-IDENTITY NOTE: eviction fails OPEN for the evicted caller (their window is dropped, so
    57	// their next call starts fresh) -- never closed. That is acceptable ONLY because the production
    58	// limiter key must be a TRUSTED identity (an authenticated token, or a gateway/WAF-provided
    59	// client id), not a spoofable header; see clientIdFromRequest's preference order + the
    60	// single-instance caveat in the file header. On a spoofable-header deployment this cap bounds
    61	// MEMORY, not abuse -- the shared-store/gateway limiter is the real abuse control.
    62	const MAX_TRACKED_KEYS = 10_000;
