   **WHY:** `1_200` is not defensible as a worst-case safety bound. The evidence is 357–368 tokens from two representative Skeptic successes and 165–252 from two judge calls: only 3.3× and 4.8× headroom. The full Skeptic calibration does not record output-token distribution, and—worse—errors on unsafe cases count as successful rejects (finding 6). The schemas permit an unbounded `reason`, the call does not set `reasoningEffort`, so GPT-OSS uses Groq’s default medium reasoning, and the installed Groq adapter omits `strict:true`, leaving JSON Schema in best-effort mode. Groq confirms that the cap includes reasoning, medium is the default, and strict mode must be explicitly requested. [Reasoning docs](https://console.groq.com/docs/reasoning), [API reference](https://console.groq.com/docs/api-reference), [Structured Outputs](https://console.groq.com/docs/structured-outputs). A length-truncated `generateObject` throws and silently becomes the exact HOLD/NO_ACTION symptom.  
   **FIX:** Set `reasoningEffort:"low"`, bound `reason` in both schemas, actually request strict JSON Schema, and catch `NoObjectGeneratedError` specifically to classify `finishReason:"length"`. Run repeated maximum-complexity/adversarial cases, record the maximum/p99 completion use, then derive the cap with explicit margin. If that requires more than 1,200, solve TPM through queuing or a higher tier instead of under-sizing the completion.

