1. **SEVERITY: High — [lib/agents/cross-family.ts:69](/Users/sharan_98/Desktop/supply-chain-ai-resilix/lib/agents/cross-family.ts:69), [lib/agents/actionops/skeptic.ts:82](/Users/sharan_98/Desktop/supply-chain-ai-resilix/lib/agents/actionops/skeptic.ts:82), [lib/evals/judge.ts:45](/Users/sharan_98/Desktop/supply-chain-ai-resilix/lib/evals/judge.ts:45)**  
   **WHY:** `1_200` is not defensible as a worst-case safety bound. The evidence is 357–368 tokens from two representative Skeptic successes and 165–252 from two judge calls: only 3.3× and 4.8× headroom. The full Skeptic calibration does not record output-token distribution, and—worse—errors on unsafe cases count as successful rejects (finding 6). The schemas permit an unbounded `reason`, the call does not set `reasoningEffort`, so GPT-OSS uses Groq’s default medium reasoning, and the installed Groq adapter omits `strict:true`, leaving JSON Schema in best-effort mode. Groq confirms that the cap includes reasoning, medium is the default, and strict mode must be explicitly requested. [Reasoning docs](https://console.groq.com/docs/reasoning), [API reference](https://console.groq.com/docs/api-reference), [Structured Outputs](https://console.groq.com/docs/structured-outputs). A length-truncated `generateObject` throws and silently becomes the exact HOLD/NO_ACTION symptom.  
   **FIX:** Set `reasoningEffort:"low"`, bound `reason` in both schemas, actually request strict JSON Schema, and catch `NoObjectGeneratedError` specifically to classify `finishReason:"length"`. Run repeated maximum-complexity/adversarial cases, record the maximum/p99 completion use, then derive the cap with explicit margin. If that requires more than 1,200, solve TPM through queuing or a higher tier instead of under-sizing the completion.

2. **SEVERITY: High — [lib/server/rate-limit.ts:38](/Users/sharan_98/Desktop/supply-chain-ai-resilix/lib/server/rate-limit.ts:38), [app/api/run-exception/route.ts:43](/Users/sharan_98/Desktop/supply-chain-ai-resilix/app/api/run-exception/route.ts:43), [lib/agents/actionops/skeptic.ts:92](/Users/sharan_98/Desktop/supply-chain-ai-resilix/lib/agents/actionops/skeptic.ts:92)**  
   **WHY:** The fix is healthy only under artificial 20-second serialization. One Skeptic request reserves about 2,621 of the documented 8,000 TPM, allowing approximately three calls/minute. The live route permits 30 requests/minute per caller and has no organization-wide Groq queue/token bucket. Thus the fourth sufficiently close live run can 429 and fail closed to HOLD; the hard outage has become a burst-dependent outage. Groq’s current free-plan table confirms the 8K TPM limit and organization-wide accounting. [Groq rate limits](https://console.groq.com/docs/rate-limits).  
   **FIX:** Add a shared, provider-aware token bucket/queue before Groq calls, coordinated across replicas and sized with meaningful margin. Align the API limit with Groq capacity, or upgrade the tier. Treat `Retry-After` as controlled deferred execution, with each attempted billable call separately budgeted and ledgered.

3. **SEVERITY: Medium — [lib/evals/judge.ts:39](/Users/sharan_98/Desktop/supply-chain-ai-resilix/lib/evals/judge.ts:39), [lib/agents/cross-family.ts:13](/Users/sharan_98/Desktop/supply-chain-ai-resilix/lib/agents/cross-family.ts:13)**  
   **WHY:** Keeping one default for two independently calibrated gates is the wrong safety trade. There is no behavioral invariant requiring Skeptic and judge to use the same model; each has different prompts, labels, thresholds, and failure costs. The coupling already propagated a model that ADR-0002 had rejected for the judge. “Preventing drift” is not stronger than preventing an unreviewed calibration invalidation. In safety-critical configuration, requiring two explicit changes is desirable friction.  
   **FIX:** Split this into `SKEPTIC_DEFAULT_MODEL` and `GROQ_JUDGE_DEFAULT_MODEL`, while sharing only provider plumbing. Attach calibration metadata/model IDs to each consumer and make a model change fail an offline guard until that consumer’s calibration record is deliberately updated.

