   **WHY:** The guard has one narrow tooth, but leaves most of the contract untested. It stays green if the script exits successfully before checking anything, the endpoint/header breaks, a role is removed, package wiring points elsewhere, env names change, or judge resolution diverges. Pricing comparison is one-way, so stale extra IDs in `PRICED` can produce a false green after the real table removes them. It also ignores `JUDGE_PROVIDER`: a Gemini judge override is incorrectly checked against Groq, while a typo such as `JUDGE_PROVIDER=grop` silently activates Gemini and the script still certifies the Groq default.  
   **FIX:** Extract an importable preflight core and test actual environment→provider→model resolution, exact pricing-set equality, role coverage, catalog decisions, and exit codes. Validate `JUDGE_PROVIDER` as a closed enum and add a subprocess test for the real CLI and package-script wiring.

